## Session Re-verification

If recon spans multiple sessions:
- Read `notes/session_log.md` before continuing
- Re-verify scope (programs update their scope)
- Re-check live hosts (infrastructure changes)
- Don't repeat completed phases — continue from where you left off

---

## Things to Do

- Run ALL passive sources before any active scanning — you need breadth first
- Check for wildcard DNS BEFORE trusting subdomain results
- Mine Wayback Machine aggressively — it's the richest source of hidden endpoints
- Analyze EVERY JavaScript file for endpoints, secrets, and source maps
- Check `robots.txt` `Disallow` entries — they're your highest-priority targets
- Crawl with authentication — many endpoints only appear when logged in
- Test old API versions — they often have weaker security
- Check non-standard ports — they often run unprotected services
- Document everything in the session log as you go — this is your memory
- Look for naming patterns in subdomains and generate permutations
- Identify the WAF early — it changes your entire testing strategy

## Things Not to Do

- Do not start active vulnerability testing before recon is complete
- Do not run noisy port scans without checking program rules first
- Do not rely on a single subdomain source — combine 5+ sources minimum
- Do not skip JavaScript analysis — it's where the best hidden attack surface lives
- Do not trust automated scanners alone — manual analysis finds what scanners miss
- Do not ignore dead subdomains — they may be takeover candidates
- Do not store actual secrets in your session log — log TYPE and LOCATION only
- Do not skip the technology profiling step — it directly guides skill selection
- Do not forget to crawl authenticated surfaces — many endpoints require login to discover

---

## Cross-Skill Links

After recon, navigate to [[skills/index.md]] and select skills based on the priority target list. The signal-to-skill mapping table above guides selection.
